    text: "The world of toys and games reflects culture, creativity, and the fundamental human need for play. Traditional toys include dolls, action figures, stuffed animals (or soft toys in British English), building blocks, and jigsaw puzzles. Board games such as chess, draughts (checkers in American English), Monopoly, Scrabble, and Trivial Pursuit remain popular across generations. Card games range from simple ones like Snap and Go Fish to complex strategy games like Bridge and Poker. Outdoor play involves swings, slides, seesaws, climbing frames, trampolines, skipping ropes, and kites. Construction toys like LEGO bricks, Meccano, and model kits encourage spatial thinking and patience. Electronic and video games have their own vast vocabulary: console, controller, joystick, level, score, multiplayer, quest, and avatar. Tabletop role-playing games use terms like character sheet, dice roll, campaign, and game master. The language of games permeates everyday English — we 'play by the rules', 'move the goalposts', 'raise the stakes', 'play our cards right', and 'level up' in life. Understanding this vocabulary connects learners to childhood memories, family traditions, and the surprisingly competitive world of modern gaming. Whether assembling a jigsaw on a rainy afternoon or battling online opponents, games unite people across ages and cultures through the shared language of play.",
